---
import ContentShell from '../layouts/ContentShell.astro'
import { loadAll, stats } from '../data/load'
import { url } from '../data/slugs'

const companies = await loadAll()
const s = await stats()
const feeds = companies.filter((c) => c.ats)
const byAts = [...new Set(feeds.map((c) => c.ats))].sort()

const description =
  'How Startup Radar collects Pune startup jobs: public ATS feeds only, refreshed every six ' +
  'hours, with the coverage limits stated plainly.'
---

<ContentShell
  title="How Startup Radar works — methodology and coverage"
  description={description}
  path={url.about()}
  h1="How this works"
  lede={description}
  crumbs={[{ name: 'About', href: url.about() }]}
  narrow
>
  <h2>Where the listings come from</h2>
  <p>
    Every role on this site is read from the company's own public job feed — the syndication
    endpoints that {byAts.join(', ')} publish so listings can be aggregated. Nothing is copied
    from LinkedIn, Naukri or Wellfound, and nothing is scraped from a page that asked not to be.
  </p>
  <p>
    A scheduled job re-reads every feed every six hours, and this site is rebuilt from the
    result. Each role shows when it was last confirmed still open.
  </p>

  <h2>What the numbers mean</h2>
  <p>
    {s.companies} Pune startups are tracked. {feeds.length} of them publish a machine-readable
    feed, which is why {s.hiring} currently show live roles and the rest show only a careers
    link. That gap is not an oversight — most Indian startups use tools with no public feed, and
    inventing listings for them would be worse than admitting the gap.
  </p>

  <h2>What we deliberately do not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We never take an application, a CV, or a fee. Apply buttons go straight to the employer.</li>
    <li>We do not rewrite job titles. What you see is what the employer published.</li>
    <li>We do not keep closed roles alive. When a role leaves the feed, its page goes with it.</li>
    <li>We do not guess at experience levels that a posting never stated.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Known limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Map pins are placed at neighbourhood centroids, not street addresses, and are nudged
      apart so overlapping companies stay visible.
    </li>
    <li>
      Experience filters read the role title and description with pattern matching. Treat them
      as a shortcut, not a guarantee.
    </li>
    <li>
      A "remote" role only appears here if it names India and no other metro. That drops some
      genuinely open roles, which we prefer to filling a Pune map with jobs in other cities.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Machine-readable</h2>
  <p>
    The full dataset is at <a href="/data/jobs.json">/data/jobs.json</a>, new roles are on
    <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>, and every page is listed in the
    <a href="/sitemap.xml">sitemap</a>. AI crawlers are welcome — see
    <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If a company is listed in the wrong area, missing entirely, or should not be here at all,
    that is a data problem worth fixing. The company list is curated by hand and is the one
    part of this site a human maintains directly.
  </p>
</ContentShell>
